.TH LOREGD 8 2026-10-19 "Peios loregd 0.21.13"
.SH NAME
loregd \- serve local Peios registry hives
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loregd
.I hive-name=database-path
.RI [ hive-name=database-path
\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B loregd
stores registry hives in SQLite and serves them to the kernel Local
Configuration Store through the Registry Source Interface (RSI).
The kernel owns access checks, namespace resolution, and layers.
The daemon implements the
.B registryd
role; peinit owns its boot service definition.
.PP
Each argument declares a hive and its absolute database path.
Arguments split at the first equals sign.
Names preserve case but compare using Unicode simple case folding.
An empty name or path, a duplicate folded name, a slash, backslash, or NUL
in a hive name, and the reserved name
.B CurrentUser
are rejected.
At least one declaration is required.
The daemon has no help, version, inspection, or recovery switches.
.PP
Startup opens or creates each hive, verifies WAL mode and schema version 1,
creates a root key if needed, and registers the hives globally.
An incompatible schema stops startup; no schema migration is performed.
The daemon reads no configuration file or registry configuration.
.PP
Volatile data uses fresh tmpfs-backed databases under
.IR /run/loregd .
Restarting the daemon discards all volatile data, including within one boot.
Ordinary reads remain available while a write transaction is open;
competing writes return busy.
Request waits use the kernel's absolute deadline.
An admitted commit completes its storage outcome even if its caller times out.
.SH SECURITY
The daemon runs as SYSTEM and requires
.B SeTcbPrivilege
for the kernel registry device.
It applies a protected SYSTEM-only descriptor to its runtime directory and
holds an exclusive lifetime lock there.
A second daemon cannot use the same runtime directory while that lock is held.
Missing database parent directories are created with mode 0700.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The registry device closed cleanly, or SIGTERM or SIGINT requested shutdown.
.TP
.B 1
Argument validation, startup preparation, device negotiation, registration,
or request-loop framing failed.
Storage errors while handling an individual request produce an RSI error
response rather than terminating the daemon.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
Unix datagram socket used for the readiness message
.BR READY=1 .
The daemon sends readiness after registering all hives and before serving.
Connection and write share a one-second deadline.
Failure is logged and serving continues.
If unset, the notification is skipped.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/pkm_registry
Kernel device for hive registration and RSI requests.
RSI lifetime extension version 1 is required.
.TP
.I /var/state/loregd/Machine.hive
Machine hive path selected by peinit.
The daemon has no built-in path default.
.TP
.I /var/state/loregd/Users.hive
Users hive path selected by peinit.
.TP
.I /run/loregd
Protected tmpfs directory containing volatile databases and the lifetime lock.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM, SIGINT
Close the device, drain requests, release transactions, and close databases.
Clean shutdown removes the per-hive volatile stores.
.SH NOTES
WAL recovery and orphan cleanup are not backup restoration.
The daemon creates no automatic startup backup and imports no Windows REGF files.
A live SQLite hive can have committed data in its WAL; the main file alone
is not a complete live backup.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peinit (8),
.BR peipkg (1)
